---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/hidden-rivers.css';
---
<Base wide title="Hidden Rivers: a 3D ocean-current atlas" description="Depth-resolved ocean currents, real seafloor relief, and Tessera coastal embeddings. An interactive research atlas by Ian Helfrich." pageClass="hidden-rivers-page">
<article class="ocean-atlas" data-surface="ocean">
  <header class="ocean-title"><div><p class="ocean-kicker">IAN HELFRICH / OCEANOGRAPHIC SYSTEMS</p><h1>Hidden Rivers</h1><p>Ocean circulation changes with depth. Follow the modeled flow through a geographically located water column.</p></div><a href="/projects/hidden-rivers">Project record</a></header>
  <nav class="ocean-tabs" aria-label="Atlas sections"><a href="#explorer">3D currents</a><a href="#coastal">Tessera coast</a><a href="#film">Animation</a><a href="#methods">Methods & sources</a></nav>
  <section id="explorer" aria-label="Interactive ocean-current atlas">
    <div class="ocean-toolbar">
      <label>Region<select id="ocean-region"><option value="agulhas">Agulhas · South Africa</option><option value="bahamas">Florida Current · Bahamas</option><option value="denmark">Denmark Strait · Irminger Sea</option></select></label>
      <label>Depth<select id="ocean-depth"><option value="all">All available layers</option><option value="0">Surface</option><option value="200">200 m</option><option value="1000">1,000 m</option></select></label>
      <label>Color<select id="ocean-palette"><option value="aurora">Aurora</option><option value="ember">Ember</option><option value="ice">Ice</option></select></label>
      <button id="ocean-play" type="button" aria-pressed="true">Pause</button><button id="ocean-reset" type="button">Reset view</button>
    </div>
    <div class="ocean-scene"><div id="ocean-canvas" aria-label="Rotatable 3D ocean-current layers and seafloor relief" role="img"></div><div class="ocean-scene-status" id="ocean-status" role="status">Loading depth-resolved ocean data…</div><div class="ocean-scene-note">Drag to orbit · Scroll to zoom<br/><span id="ocean-vertical">Vertical scale ×80</span></div><div class="ocean-depth-labels" id="ocean-labels"></div><div class="ocean-legend"><span>Horizontal speed · m/s</span><div id="ocean-gradient"></div><span class="ocean-legend-ticks"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span>2+</span></span></div></div>
    <div class="ocean-controls"><label class="ocean-time">Model date <output id="ocean-date">29 Sep 2026, 00:00 UTC</output><input aria-label="Model time" id="ocean-time" type="range" min="0" max="432000" step="900" value="0"/></label><label>Vertical exaggeration <output id="ocean-exag-label">80×</output><input aria-label="Vertical exaggeration" id="ocean-exag" type="range" min="1" max="200" value="80"/></label></div>
    <p class="ocean-caption">HYCOM/ESPC analysis, 29 September–4 October 2026. Particles follow horizontal velocity on fixed depth planes. The displayed vertical spacing is exaggerated; no vertical particle velocity is inferred. Dark gaps include land, shallow seabed, and missing data.</p>
    <p id="ocean-region-note" class="ocean-caption">Agulhas: compare the surface, 200 m, and 1,000 m layers. The same horizontal location can carry different currents at different depths.</p>
    <noscript><p>JavaScript is required for the 3D explorer. The video and methods below remain available.</p></noscript>
  </section>
  <section class="ocean-essay" id="coastal"><div><p class="ocean-kicker">COASTAL CONTEXT / TESSERA v1.1</p><h2>The coast supplies a different kind of evidence.</h2><p>This 5.12 km patch of the Cape Peninsula uses actual 2024 Tessera embeddings at 10 m sampling. Three principal components compress 128 learned features into a color image. Similar colors indicate similarity in that chosen representation; they do not label habitats without validation.</p><p>The annual coastal representation and the daily ocean analysis remain separate layers. Tessera contributes spatial context. HYCOM supplies the velocity field. Coloring the two attractively does not establish a statistical relationship between them, and these dates do not support a simultaneous coastal–ocean comparison.</p></div><figure><img src="/hidden-rivers/images/tessera.png" alt="Three principal components of real Tessera coastal embeddings near Cape Peninsula" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>18.40° E, 34.20° S · 2024 · 128 → 3 components<br/>RGB channels use separate 2nd–98th percentile stretches.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section id="film" class="ocean-film"><p class="ocean-kicker">THREE REGIONS / SIX DAILY SNAPSHOTS</p><h2>Five ocean days, seen in motion.</h2><video controls preload="none" playsinline poster="/hidden-rivers/images/agulhas-poster.png"><source src="/hidden-rivers/Hidden_Rivers_Atlas.mp4" type="video/mp4"/><track kind="captions" src="/hidden-rivers/captions.vtt" srclang="en" label="English" default/>Your browser does not support this video.</video><p class="ocean-caption">The 60-second film shows Florida/Bahamas at 200 m, Agulhas at 200 m, and Denmark Strait/Irminger Sea at 1,000 m. These are numerical tracers, continuously reseeded to keep the field visible. The color scale changes between chapters and is labeled in each.</p><a href="/hidden-rivers/Hidden_Rivers_Atlas.mp4" download>Download the animation</a></section>
  <section class="ocean-reading" id="methods"><p class="ocean-kicker">PHYSICS, MEASUREMENT, AND THE LIMITS OF THE PICTURE</p><h2>What makes an underwater current?</h2><p>Water accelerates when forces fail to balance. Pressure differences can arise from a sloping sea surface and from density differences within the water column. Rotation turns the flow, wind supplies momentum at the surface, and boundaries and seafloor topography redirect it. Mixing transfers heat, salt, and momentum across layers. Temperature and salinity help determine density, but a map of density alone is not a complete map of velocity.</p><p>The phrase “underwater river” covers several mechanisms. A narrow boundary current, a rotating eddy, a dense overflow, and a sediment-laden turbidity current can all look river-like. They require different observations and different models. In particular, the Denmark Strait overflow descends along topography after crossing a sill. A horizontal slice at 1,000 m only samples part of the surrounding circulation; it cannot reconstruct that descent.</p><h3>What this animation computes</h3><p>Each particle samples eastward and northward velocity from four neighboring valid cells, with linear interpolation between daily snapshots. A midpoint integration step advances longitude and latitude using their physical length at the particle’s latitude. Integration steps are capped at 900 seconds. Land and missing-data boundaries terminate the visible segment and trigger reseeding. Tracers remain at their selected depth; their brightness and number have no interpretation as water volume, transport, or probability.</p><p>The browser uses velocity rounded to 0.001 m/s, matching the source packing precision. The requested spatial stride produces a 0.16° longitude × 0.08° latitude grid. These are sampled instantaneous analyses, not daily means. Smoothing creates a readable visualization without adding resolving power. Six daily samples cannot recover short-period tides or validate detailed coastal passages.</p><h3>Three-dimensional geography</h3><p>The scene converts longitude and latitude to local distances using an equirectangular approximation at the region’s central latitude. Depth is measured downward from the surface. ETOPO1 relief is subsampled to 0.2° for the terrain mesh, so small channels and steep slopes are simplified. The exaggeration control multiplies vertical distances only. Current trajectories follow the ocean model’s wet mask; the independently gridded relief may differ near steep boundaries. Satellite imagery is contextual and combines acquisition dates.</p><h3>How this improves on the “hidden rivers” story</h3><p>The <a href="https://discoverwildscience.com/the-oceans-hidden-rivers-how-water-flows-beneath-the-sea-2-373648/">article that prompted this atlas</a> treats polar sinking as the dominant explanation for much of circulation and describes layers as essentially unmixed. That account needs qualification. Wind-driven circulation, buoyancy changes, tides, and mixing all matter; their relative roles depend on the current and timescale. The Gulf Stream belongs to both a wind-driven gyre and the larger Atlantic overturning system. A “conveyor belt” is a useful sketch, but it cannot replace a depth-resolved, time-varying velocity field.</p><p>The next research step is observational validation: compare model velocity against suitably colocated ADCP or current-meter records, and compare temperature and salinity profiles against Argo or CTD observations. Density calculated with TEOS-10, vertical sections across an overflow, and transport integrated across a defined section would allow stronger physical claims. Those validation and transport calculations are not included in this release.</p><h3>Sources and reproducibility</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.hycom.org/dataserver/espc-d-v02/global-analysis">HYCOM/ESPC-D-V02</a>: depth-resolved eastward and northward velocity. The application bundles fixed subsets and does not silently refresh.</li><li><a href="https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/etopo180.html">NOAA ETOPO1</a>: contextual seafloor elevation. This mesh is not a high-resolution survey.</li><li><a href="https://geotessera.org/">Tessera</a>: v1.1 dClimate coastal embeddings, 2024, accessed using GeoTessera. Embeddings are CC0.</li><li>Context imagery: Esri World Imagery, Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics and the GIS User Community.</li><li><a href="https://www.whoi.edu/ocean-learning-hub/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-circulation/currents-gyres-eddies/">WHOI: currents, gyres and eddies</a>; <a href="https://www.gfdl.noaa.gov/ocean-mixing/">NOAA/GFDL: ocean mixing</a>; <a href="https://www.nodc.noaa.gov/archive/arc0001/0000649/1.1/data/0-data/disk1/cmdac/acm8a/acm8a.htm">Denmark Strait current-meter observations</a>.</li></ul><p><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/manifest.json">Machine-readable data manifest</a> · <a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests.json">Exact download requests</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/ihelfrich.github.io/tree/main/scripts/hidden-rivers">Analysis and rendering code</a> · <a href="/projects/oceanographic-systems">Related oceanographic work</a></p><p class="ocean-caption">Research visualization, released 6 October 2026. Model output is not direct observation. No ecological, acoustic-propagation, navigational, or causal conclusion is established by the animation.</p></section>
</article>
<script>import { startOceanAtlas } from '../scripts/hidden-rivers/atlas.js'; startOceanAtlas();</script>

</Base>
